Page Object Models trade off clarity for encapsulation. Concrete example [1]. They can make tests look &quot;cleaner&quot; but often obscure what&#x27;s actually happening. For example:<p><pre><code>    await page.getStarted(); &#x2F;&#x2F; what does this actually do?
</code></pre>
vs<p><pre><code>    await page.locator(&#x27;a&#x27;, { hasText: &#x27;Get started&#x27; }).first().click();
    await expect(page.locator(&#x27;h1&#x27;, { hasText: &#x27;Installation&#x27; })).toBeVisible();
</code></pre>
The second version is explicit and self-documenting. Tests don&#x27;t always benefit from aggressive DRY, but I&#x27;ve seen teams adopt POMs to coordinate between SDETs and SWEs.<p>--<p>1: <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;playwright.dev&#x2F;docs&#x2F;pom" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;playwright.dev&#x2F;docs&#x2F;pom</a>

